    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Sea ice microalgae represent the base of the marine food web in the Arctic Ocean, providing essential carbon and nutrients to primary consumers. Their abundance and community composition are likely to change in a warm and melting Arctic, affecting the quantity and quality of this food source. In this study, we investigate the summer (June – July) sea ice characteristics of pack ice and sea ice microalgae community composition at 11 stations in the Chukchi Sea. We found that snow depth has the most significant relationship with community composition of sea ice microalgae, likely due to its strong attenuation of light, restricting light access to bottom communities. The species Nitzschia frigida dominated most communities, and other groups showed little variation associated to snow depth, suggesting that these groups have a high level of adaptability to light and other environmental conditions. Certain groups, such as Amphiprora sp., centric diatoms and Melosira arctica, were significantly associated with the thinnest snow depths, suggesting a higher light requirement for these cells, but also likely lower rates of photoinhibition from the highest light levels. In a warmer Arctic with decreased snow depth, we will likely see shifts in community composition toward these low snow, high light-associated groups, altering community composition, and changing the food source of primary consumers.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Palm, A. (2024). Biology of Arctic Sea Ice: Investigating Microalgae with Varying Sea Ice Characteristics. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/kj652qd7014. https://doi.org/10.25740/kj652qd7014.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
